@model VoteButtonModel
@{
    // The button carries an explicit intent instead of toggling, so a repeated submission
    // asks the server for the same end state rather than undoing the previous one.
    string handler = VotingPageModel.VoteHandlerName;
    string label = UiText.VoteButton;

    if (Model.HasVoted)
    {
        handler = VotingPageModel.UnvoteHandlerName;
        label = UiText.VotedButton;
    }

    string buttonId = $"voto-{Model.IdeaId}";
    string titleId = $"ideia-titulo-{Model.IdeaId}";
}
<form method="post" data-vote-form data-idea-id="@Model.IdeaId" asp-page-handler="@handler">
    <input type="hidden" name="ideaId" value="@Model.IdeaId" />
    @*
        The spoken name of this button is its own wording followed by the title of the idea
        it votes on, because on the ranking every one of these buttons reads as the same
        single word. Somebody moving through the page button by button would hear "Votar"
        once per idea with nothing to tell them apart, while somebody looking at the screen
        has the heading directly above and never meets the problem. That is what makes this
        one worth guarding: it cannot be caught by looking.

        Both pages that render this partial put the id named here on their title element.
        The button names itself first so that its visible wording stays the beginning of the
        spoken name, which is what keeps the two the same thing. If a future caller renders
        the partial with no such element, the missing reference is ignored and the name falls
        back to the wording alone, which is where this started.
    *@
    <button type="submit"
            id="@buttonId"
            data-vote-button
            aria-labelledby="@buttonId @titleId">@label</button>
    <span data-vote-count aria-live="polite">@UiText.FormatVoteCount(Model.VoteCount)</span>
</form>
